\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces  External validation on N-BaIoT (Danmini doorbell device, 3 seeds, 39 windows per attack class, 7 benign-validation windows). \textsc  {HNIDS}{} (condition D) is trained solely on CICIoT2023 with analyst-specified hard negatives; no N-BaIoT data is used at any stage of training or scaler fitting. The threshold is calibrated on a held-out N-BaIoT benign validation set. Attack types are Mirai and BASHLITE (gafgyt) botnet variants distinct from the CICIoT2023 training distribution. \textbf  {AUC $<$ 0.5 indicates inverted score polarity}; the detector assigns lower anomaly scores to attack traffic than to benign traffic under the cross-dataset distribution shift, confirming that zero-shot transfer from CICIoT2023 to N-BaIoT is poor. FPR\,=\,1.00 across all rows reflects the degenerate decision boundary arising from this score collapse. }}{14}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:nbaiot}{{8}{14}{External validation on N-BaIoT (Danmini doorbell device, 3 seeds, 39 windows per attack class, 7 benign-validation windows). \ours {} (condition D) is trained solely on CICIoT2023 with analyst-specified hard negatives; no N-BaIoT data is used at any stage of training or scaler fitting. The threshold is calibrated on a held-out N-BaIoT benign validation set. Attack types are Mirai and BASHLITE (gafgyt) botnet variants distinct from the CICIoT2023 training distribution. \textbf {AUC $<$ 0.5 indicates inverted score polarity}; the detector assigns lower anomaly scores to attack traffic than to benign traffic under the cross-dataset distribution shift, confirming that zero-shot transfer from CICIoT2023 to N-BaIoT is poor. FPR\,=\,1.00 across all rows reflects the degenerate decision boundary arising from this score collapse}{table.caption.9}{}}
